WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.032 --> 00:00:08.370
Hand in hand, Christine and Raoul rushed downstairs from the roof, stopping on the eighth floor of the

2
00:00:08.371 --> 00:00:18.749
opera house. As they started running through a corridor, a mysterious figure blocked their way. He pointed

3
00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:23.984
in another direction. "Go that way! Hurry!" "Who was that?" Raoul glanced behind them as Christine dragged him

4
00:00:23.985 --> 00:00:31.068
away. "The Persian! He's always wandering around the opera house, but no one knows much about him. Come

5
00:00:31.069 --> 00:00:37.896
on!" Christine found another staircase and led Raoul to her dressing room. She locked the door. "Erik

6
00:00:37.897 --> 00:00:45.051
has a powerful hold over you," Raoul said. "Are you sure you'll be ready to elope with me

7
00:00:45.052 --> 00:00:55.331
tomorrow night?" Christine nodded. "After listening to my performance, Erik will be waiting for me in his

8
00:00:55.332 --> 00:01:00.116
house by the lake. It'll be the perfect time to escape." "And how were you supposed to get

9
00:01:01.343 --> 00:01:07.319
to Erik's house—through your mirror?" "No," Christine said. "I don't know how to use the mirror as a

10
00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:15.586
passageway." She opened a box and removed an enormous key. "But with this key, I can go

11
00:01:15.587 --> 00:01:18.815
outside and open some gates. They lead to an underground passage and the lake." Christine held up the

12
00:01:18.816 --> 00:01:26.874
key, her eyes widening in horror as she stared at her left hand. "Oh no!" "What's wrong, darling?"

13
00:01:26.875 --> 00:01:37.305
"I've lost Erik's ring! He said, 'I give you this ring and your freedom on the condition

14
00:01:37.306 --> 00:01:43.277
that the ring remains on your finger. As long as you wear it, you'll be protected against all

15
00:01:43.278 --> 00:01:48.957
danger, and I'll remain your friend. But woe to you if you ever lose it!' Oh, Raoul, where

16
00:01:48.958 --> 00:01:54.726
is it?" The couple searched every inch of Christine's dressing room but couldn't find the ring. Christine

17
00:01:55.618 --> 00:02:03.864
was growing more frantic by the minute. "It must have slipped off my finger on the roof or

18
00:02:03.865 --> 00:02:12.281
while we were running downstairs. Oh, terrible things are going to happen!" "Let's leave at once," Raoul insisted,

19
00:02:12.282 --> 00:02:21.251
taking Christine's hands in his. "No," Christine protested, pulling her hands away. "I must sing for Erik

20
00:02:21.252 --> 00:02:32.254
one last time tomorrow night. It's the least I can do for him because it'll break his heart

21
00:02:32.255 --> 00:02:38.704
when I run away with you. Go home, Raoul! I'll be waiting here tomorrow night at midnight."

22
00:02:38.705 --> 00:02:44.792
Reluctantly Raoul went home, greatly upset by all that Christine had said. He was still thinking about the

23
00:02:44.793 --> 00:02:51.467
situation when he went downstairs for breakfast the next morning. His brother, Philippe, slapped a newspaper down on

24
00:02:52.977 --> 00:03:02.540
the table and pointed to a short item. The latest rumor is that Mademoiselle Christine Daaé, the

25
00:03:02.541 --> 00:03:10.891
opera singer, is engaged to Raoul, Viscount de Chagny. We hear that a certain count is not rejoicing

26
00:03:10.892 --> 00:03:21.280
over his younger brother's happy announcement and has vowed to prevent the marriage. Will this tale end with

27
00:03:21.281 --> 00:03:32.140
the viscount leading his love to the altar, or will it, like so many operas, have a

28
00:03:32.267 --> 00:03:45.081
sad and tragic ending? "What's going on, Raoul?" Philippe demanded. "All of Paris is talking about you and

29
00:03:45.082 --> 00:03:51.203
. . . and that woman! You're making our family look ridiculous!" It was impossible for Raoul to

30
00:03:51.204 --> 00:03:54.710
explain this situation to Philippe, but he tried his best. "If I don't save Christine from Erik,

31
00:03:55.546 --> 00:04:01.142
she'll be lost forever. So I will save her." "Who is this Erik?" "My rival, who lives in

32
00:04:01.143 --> 00:04:08.322
an opera house cellar near the lake and—" "What are you babbling about?" Philippe snapped. "Never mind.

33
00:04:08.323 --> 00:04:13.181
I'm going to elope with Christine tonight. Good-bye, Philippe." "If you try anything so foolish, I'll know how

34
00:04:13.182 --> 00:04:21.734
to stop you!" Philippe said as Raoul strode from the room. For the rest of the day, Raoul

35
00:04:21.735 --> 00:04:30.425
devoted himself to his preparations. The horses, the carriage, the coachman, the luggage, the food and money

36
00:04:30.426 --> 00:04:41.850
for the journey—everything had to be arranged. At nine o'clock Raoul's carriage took its place in a line

37
00:04:41.851 --> 00:04:48.312
of horse-drawn vehicles outside the opera house, including his brother's. The curtains in Raoul's carriage were drawn so

38
00:04:48.313 --> 00:04:56.019
he didn't see a shadow in a long black coat and soft felt hat pass along the

39
00:04:57.142 --> 00:05:04.084
sidewalk. The figure examined Raoul's carriage and horses before slipping off into the darkness. Some people later said

40
00:05:04.085 --> 00:05:10.358
they thought the shadow was Raoul, although he usually wore a top hat to the opera. Others speculated

41
00:05:10.359 --> 00:05:16.940
that the shadow was the phantom of the opera. Raoul entered the theater and took a seat

42
00:05:16.941 --> 00:05:23.127
near the stage without looking up at Philippe's box. The opera that night was Faust , and Christine

43
00:05:23.154 --> 00:05:32.318
was scheduled to sing the role of Marguerite. Meanwhile the audience was abuzz with talk of the

44
00:05:32.319 --> 00:05:40.087
engagement announcement in the newspaper. "Christine Daaé is aiming rather high for a peasant girl who happens to

45
00:05:40.088 --> 00:05:47.962
have a nice voice," one woman said to another. Her friend looked up at Philippe's box, where the

46
00:05:47.963 --> 00:05:57.376
count sat alone. "I wonder where the viscount is tonight." As the opera started, Philippe kept his

47
00:05:58.309 --> 00:06:03.455
eyes on the stage, ignoring the people staring at him and gossiping. From the moment Christine stepped onstage,

48
00:06:03.456 --> 00:06:12.138
she sensed some antagonism from the audience, and her confidence wavered. "I hope she doesn't start to croak,"

49
00:06:12.139 --> 00:06:26.007
one man whispered to his wife. Just then Carlotta herself made a sensational entrance as she took

50
00:06:26.008 --> 00:06:31.282
a seat at the front of one of the boxes. Poor Christine raised her eyes and recognized her

51
00:06:31.283 --> 00:06:37.405
rival at once. However, instead of intimidating the young singer, Carlotta inspired Christine to triumph once again. From

52
00:06:37.406 --> 00:06:45.934
that moment Christine sang with all her heart and soul. Christine sang with her arms outstretched, the

53
00:06:45.935 --> 00:06:52.208
glory of the music inspiring her. Seeing her looking so beautiful and singing so wonderfully, Raoul stood up,

54
00:06:52.209 --> 00:06:57.508
facing his beloved. Christine went on. At that moment the stage was plunged into darkness. It all happened

55
00:06:58.235 --> 00:07:02.902
so quickly that the audience barely had time to react. At once the gas was relit, and

56
00:07:02.903 --> 00:07:10.648
the stage was illuminated again. But Christine was nowhere to be seen. It was as if an angel

57
00:07:10.649 --> 00:07:16.877
had really carried her off! Raoul uttered a cry of agony, while Philippe sprang to his feet.

58
00:07:16.878 --> 00:07:25.621
People looked from the stage to Philippe to Raoul and wondered if this curious incident were connected to

59
00:07:25.622 --> 00:07:33.054
the newspaper story. The curtain dropped, and both men left their seats while the rest of the audience

60
00:07:33.055 --> 00:07:38.453
waited for some news. At last the curtain rose again, and the conductor stepped onstage. "Ladies and

61
00:07:38.454 --> 00:07:48.873
gentleman, something terrible has happened—our leading lady has disappeared before our eyes, and nobody knows where she is!"

